.TH ZERODDS-XRCE-AGENTD.YAML 5 "" "ZeroDDS" "ZeroDDS Manual"
.SH NAME
zerodds-xrce-agentd.yaml \- configuration file for zerodds-xrce-agentd(1)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I /etc/zerodds/xrce-agentd.yaml
.SH DESCRIPTION
YAML document describing how the DDS-XRCE agent daemon listens for Micro
Clients and maps their sessions onto DDS. This config schema is a ZeroDDS
extension \(em DDS-XRCE 1.0 does not define an on-disk agent configuration
format. The parser that reads it is
.IR crates/xrce-agent/src/daemon/config.rs::DaemonConfig::from_config .
.PP
Top-level keys:
.BR log_level ", " vendor_id ", " session_timeout_secs ", "
.BR limits ", " heartbeat ", " upstream ", " discovery ", " authorized_client_keys ", "
.BR required_client_properties ", " access_rules ", " client_applications ", "
.BR listeners ", " type_files ", " xml_profile_files " and " qos_library_files .
.PP
An unknown top-level key, or an unknown key inside a known section, stops
the daemon from starting; the error names the file and line.
.PP
.BI ${VAR} " and " ${VAR:-default}
are substituted from the environment before the document is parsed.
.SH KEYS
.SS Top level
.TP
.B log_level
.IR trace " | " debug " | " info " | " warn " | " error .
.TP
.B vendor_id
This agent's
.I XrceVendorId
as
.IR "\(dqmajor.minor\(dq" ,
each 0\(en255, e.g.
.I \(dq1.15\(dq
for eProsima's own registered agent id. Default
.I \(dq0.0\(dq
(none).
.TP
.B session_timeout_secs
Seconds of client inactivity \(em no message received on any stream \(em
before the session and every object it created are dropped. Default
.IR 60 .
.SS limits
.TP
.B max_clients
Maximum concurrent client sessions. Default
.IR 1024 .
.TP
.B max_objects_per_client
Maximum DDS-XRCE objects (participants, topics, publishers, subscribers,
writers, readers, types, QoS profiles) one client session may CREATE at
this agent. Default
.IR 256 .
For a client routed to an upstream agent
.RB ( upstream: )
this agent creates no object; the limits of the upstream agent apply.
.PP
The DATA the agent holds for a reliable output stream of a client are
bounded by the sender window of the stream: 16 messages sent and not
acknowledged (DDS-XRCE 1.0 \(sc8.4.14). A read on a reliable stream takes a
sample out of its DDS DataReader only while the window has room; the other
samples stay in the DataReader, whose History QoS decides which it keeps
(KEEP_LAST depth, or KEEP_ALL within its resource limits). The client's
ACKNACK that frees the window takes the next samples at once. A sample larger
than the client's MTU goes out in FRAGMENTs; those the window has no room for
wait for the ACKNACKs. A READ_DATA without delivery_control makes its one read
call when the stream has room, for as many samples as the room; the others
stay in the DataReader for the next READ_DATA. A best-effort stream holds
nothing for an acknowledgement and is not bounded this way.
.PP
.B max_pending_samples
is no longer read: a file that sets it stops the daemon with an error that
names the key. It bounded a queue of the library interface
.RB ( SessionEngine::submit_sample )
that the daemon never fills.
.SS heartbeat
HEARTBEATs of the agent on its reliable output streams (DDS-XRCE 1.0
\(sc8.3.5.12). The specification dictates no timing and expects the agent to
send HEARTBEATs only when it has an indication that the client is active and
not in a sleep cycle. The agent counts a client as active on a stream for
.B client_active_window_ms
after it last heard the client or sent a new message on that stream; a
retransmission does not count. A client heard again after a silence gets the
HEARTBEATs of its streams with unacknowledged messages with the answer to
its message.
.TP
.B period_ms
Milliseconds between two HEARTBEATs of a stream with unacknowledged
messages, 1 or more. Default
.IR 500 .
.TP
.B client_active_window_ms
Milliseconds a client counts as active. Default
.IR 2000 .
.TP
.B silent_client
.I suppress
(default): no HEARTBEAT to a client silent for longer than the window.
.IR send :
the period also for a silent client, until the session times out.
.SS upstream
Federation (DDS-XRCE 1.0 \(sc10.4): this agent is a client of another
agent for the clients it routes there. For each routed client it opens a
session at the upstream agent with the client's ClientKey, session_id and
properties, and relays CREATE, DELETE, WRITE_DATA, READ_DATA and GET_INFO
unchanged on the stream they came on; STATUS, INFO and DATA of the upstream
agent go back to the client on their stream. This agent creates no DDS
entity for a routed client. While the upstream agent cannot be reached the
requests wait (store and forward); a lost session is opened again and its
objects and running reads are created again, and the requests the upstream
agent had neither acknowledged nor answered are sent again after them, in
their order (a request whose acknowledgement was lost with the link may be
carried out twice, none is lost). A FRAGMENT run goes upstream whole; the
part the sender window has no room for follows as the upstream agent
acknowledges. The limits of the upstream agent apply to the objects of a
routed client; of this agent's
.B limits
only
.B max_clients
applies to it. The answers of the upstream agent on a reliable stream go to
the client as far as the client's sender window on that stream has room;
this agent takes no more of them from the link and leaves them there
unacknowledged, so the window of the upstream agent fills and it takes no
more samples out of its DataReaders for the client. The client's ACKNACK
hands over what waited and acknowledges it to the upstream agent at once.
While the link holds answers it sends no keepalive (below); the HEARTBEATs of
the upstream agent for the held messages keep the link heard.
The first session of a client at the upstream agent replaces any session the
upstream agent may still hold for it (one an earlier run of this agent left
there, or one whose DELETE was lost): a CREATE_CLIENT with the other
session_id goes first, then one with the client's. A RESET of a routed client
ends its reads at the upstream agent: each gets a READ_DATA with max_samples
0, and a DATA of them still on its way does not reach the client. Without the
block there is no federation.
.TP
.B locator
.IR udp://host:port " or " tcp://host:port .
Required. Other schemes are refused.
.TP
.B mtu
The MTU this agent asks for at the upstream agent and splits its requests
into FRAGMENTs by. Default
.IR 508 ;
the smallest is
.I 13
(a message header with a ClientKey and a FRAGMENT submessage header leave
one octet of FRAGMENT data); a smaller value is refused, and so is one
larger than one message of the link (65\|507 over UDP and IPv4, 65\|527
over UDP and IPv6).
.TP
.B keepalive_ms
Milliseconds between two GET_INFO of the upstream agent while a session is
open and the link holds no answer the client has no room for. Default
.IR 1000 .
.TP
.B link_timeout_ms
Milliseconds without a message of the upstream agent after which the link
counts as lost and is opened again; longer than
.BR keepalive_ms .
Default
.IR 5000 .
.TP
.B reconnect_ms
Milliseconds before a new attempt after a failed one. Default
.IR 1000 .
.TP
.B max_queued_messages
Requests one client's link holds while the upstream agent cannot take them;
a request beyond is answered STATUS_ERR_RESOURCES. Default
.IR 256 .
.TP
.B clients
List of ClientKeys
.RI ( 0xAABBCCDD )
routed upstream; without it all clients are. A client routed upstream must
not have
.BR client_applications ,
and no
.B access_rules
entry may cover it (see below).
.PP
A link over
.I tcp://
is connected, written and read by threads of its own, never while the agent
handles a message of a client: an upstream agent that drops the connection
attempt or stops reading delays no other client. What it does not read
waits in the link up to 1\|MiB beyond the socket's send buffer; past that the
requests wait in the queue
.RB ( max_queued_messages ).
A link that hears nothing for
.B link_timeout_ms
is opened again on a new connection; a connection attempt gives up after
.BR link_timeout_ms .
A connected link that is given up (the session of its client ended, or it
is opened again) writes what it was handed, the DELETE of the session among
it, and then ends the connection, within 1\|s; one whose connection was not
made sends nothing.
.PP
A best-effort or STREAMID_NONE request larger than one message of the link
(65\|507 octets over UDP and IPv4, 65\|527 over UDP and IPv6, 65\|535 over
TCP) cannot be split into FRAGMENTs and is answered STATUS_ERR_RESOURCES.
.SS "authorized_client_keys, required_client_properties, access_rules"
The AccessController (DDS-XRCE 1.0 \(sc7.4). Without any of them the
agent restricts nothing.
.TP
.B authorized_client_keys
List of ClientKeys
.RI ( 0xAABBCCDD ).
A CREATE_CLIENT with another key is answered STATUS_ERR_DENIED
(\(sc7.8.2.1). Empty or absent: every key.
.TP
.B required_client_properties
List of
.BR name: / value:
pairs every CREATE_CLIENT has to carry; otherwise STATUS_ERR_DENIED.
.TP
.B access_rules
List of rules, evaluated in order; the first that matches an operation
decides it. Each has
.BR effect: " " allow | deny
and may narrow it with
.B client
(a ClientKey; absent: every client),
.B operations
.RI ( create ", " update ", " get_info ", " delete ", " write_data ", " read_data ),
.B kinds
.RI ( participant ", " topic ", " publisher ", " subscriber ", " datawriter ,
.IR datareader ", " type ", " qosprofile ", " application ),
.B domains
(domain ids) and
.B topics
(names; a trailing
.I *
matches a prefix). As soon as one rule exists, an operation no rule matches
is answered STATUS_ERR_DENIED.
.PP
The keys and properties apply to every client: this agent answers the
CREATE_CLIENT. The rules do not apply to a client routed upstream
.RB ( upstream: ):
its operations go to the upstream agent unchanged, and the access rules of
that agent decide them; it holds the client's objects and sees its ClientKey
and properties. A rule that names a routed client, or a rule without
.B client
while any client is routed, stops the daemon from starting.
.SS discovery
Multicast
.B GET_INFO
responder (DDS-XRCE 1.0 \(sc11.2.4): a Micro Client without a hardcoded
agent address finds this daemon here.
.TP
.B enabled
Boolean. Default
.IR true .
.TP
.B port
UDP port of the discovery multicast group. Default
.I 7400
(XRCE_DISCOVERY_PORT).
.SS listeners (list)
At least one entry is required; the default (when the key is entirely
absent) is a single
.I udp
listener on
.IR 0.0.0.0:2018 ,
eProsima's own default agent port. An entry is either a map with
.B kind
and the keys below, or one string
.IR udp:host:port ,
.I tcp:host:port
or
.I serial:device
(115200 baud) \(em the form
.B \-\-listen
writes.
.TP
.B kind
.IR udp " | " tcp " | " serial .
.TP
.B bind
.IR "host:port" .
Used by
.IR udp " and " tcp .
Default
.I 0.0.0.0:2018
for either.
.I tcp
frames messages with the DDS-XRCE \(sc11.3 2-byte length prefix.
.TP
.B device
Serial device path, e.g.
.IR /dev/ttyUSB0 .
Required for
.IR kind: \ serial ,
which needs the daemon's
.I serial
build feature and frames messages per DDS-XRCE Annex C. The packaged
systemd unit's
.B PrivateDevices
hardening blocks the device node; a serial deployment must override the
unit.
.TP
.B baud_rate
Integer baud rate for
.IR kind: \ serial .
Default
.IR 115200 .
.SS type_files (list)
Precompiled
.I TypeObject
values (XCDR2, as
.I TypeObject::to_bytes_le
produces \(em
.BR zerodds-idlc " or " zerodds-wire-probe
build one ahead of time), COMPLETE, one per file. A topic whose type name
matches an entry is created with that type for every client: key hashes,
dispose/unregister and the type announced to DDS peers. A client's own
OBJK_TYPE of the same name takes precedence for that client. The daemon
does not start if a file does not load, is not COMPLETE, or describes a
type other than its
.BR name .
Clients that send no type (the eProsima Micro-XRCE-DDS examples) need an
entry for keyed topics; without one a topic is forwarded opaque and
unkeyed.
.TP
.B name
The DDS type name this
.I TypeObject
is registered under.
.TP
.B file
Path to the binary
.IR TypeObject .
.SS xml_profile_files (list of strings)
Fast DDS profile files, the form the eProsima Micro-XRCE-DDS Agent reads
with
.BR -r :
a
.I <profiles>
root, or
.I <dds>
around
.IR <profiles> ", " <types> " and " <qos_library> .
A
.I REPRESENTATION_BY_REFERENCE
CREATE names the
.I profile_name
of a
.IR <participant> ", " <topic> ", " <publisher> ", " <subscriber> ", "
.IR <data_writer> " or " <data_reader>
there, which is read like the same element sent inline. A reference to a
profile no file defines is refused with
.IR STATUS_ERR_UNKNOWN_REFERENCE .
.I <types>
define topic types for every client, like
.BR type_files .
Other elements (
.IR <requester> ,
.IR <transport_descriptors> ,
\&...) are named on stderr at start and not used. The daemon does not
start if a file does not parse, defines a
.I profile_name
twice for one element, or a type twice.
.SS qos_library_files (list of strings)
DDS-XML 1.0
.I <qos_library>
documents. An attribute-form representation's
.I qos_profile=\(dqLibrary::Profile\(dq
names one of their profiles, resolved with its
.I base_name
chain; its DataWriter or DataReader QoS (and the Publisher or Subscriber
QoS for PARTITION and GROUP_DATA) apply before the representation's own
attributes. Policies the agent does not apply are named in the log.
.SH RELOAD
SIGHUP to
.BR zerodds-xrce-agentd (1)
re-reads this file (and its
.I xrce-agentd.d/
drop-ins) and applies
.BR limits.max_clients ", " limits.max_objects_per_client " and "
.B session_timeout_secs
without a restart. Every other key that changed, among them
.BR listeners: ", " type_files: ", " xml_profile_files: ", " heartbeat.*
and
.BR upstream.* ,
is named in the log and ignored until the daemon is restarted.
.SH EXAMPLES
See
.IR /usr/share/zerodds/configs/xrce-agentd.yaml.example .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR zerodds-xrce-agentd (1),
DDS-XRCE 1.0 (OMG formal/2023-03-01), DDS-XML.
